from pathlib import Path
import json
p=Path('content/networks/02-ip-routing/ip-nat-ipv6.questions.json');q=json.loads(p.read_text())
q[0]['prompt']+=' Use the lesson\'s one-protocol toy model: no local service, fallback host or other inbound rule.'
q[0]['options'][0]['explanation']='The stated toy model has no broadcast fallback: it returns only an existing mapping.'
q[0]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct under the specified model. Real translation keys and local-service handling can be more complex than this port-only lookup.'
q[0]['options'][3]['explanation']='The scenario excludes a local service or configured rule for that port. An unmatched arrival does not allocate an internal destination in this model.'
q[0]['workedExample']='For the fixed public address and single transport protocol, look up port 8080. Only 40000 and 40001 exist, so the toy returns None and drops the packet. Real routers may separately have local services, static mappings, filtering or fallback rules; those are explicitly excluded here.'
q[1]['options'][0]['explanation']=q[1]['options'][0]['explanation'].replace('six zero groups','five zero groups')
q[1]['workedExample']='1. Remove leading zeros: `2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1`.\n2. Groups 3 through 7 are five zero groups.\n3. Compress that longest run: `2001:db8::1`.\n4. Expanding gives two initial groups, five zero groups and the final 1: eight groups total.'
q[2]['options'][0]['explanation']='The fe80::/10 prefix is link-local, not an ISP global-unicast allocation. Global unicast has more nuance than a single exhaustive 2000::/3 type test.'
q[2]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct. Routers must not forward packets with link-local source or destination onto other links.'
q[2]['workedExample']=q[2]['workedExample'].replace('| 2000::/3 | Global unicast |','| 2000::/3 | Main global-unicast allocation block |').replace('also hints that the interface identifier was built with modified EUI-64 from a MAC address.','is compatible with a MAC-derived modified EUI-64 identifier, but does not prove how it was generated.')
q[3]['prompt']='Using documentation addresses for a hypothetical delegation of `2001:db8:ab00::/56`, how many /64 subnets fit?'
q[3]['workedExample']=q[3]['workedExample'].replace('`2001:db8:abff::/64`','`2001:db8:ab00:ff::/64`')
q[4]['workedExample']=q[4]['workedExample'].replace('Modern systems avoid this because it exposes the MAC; they use RFC 7217 stable privacy IDs and temporary addresses.','Opaque stable and temporary interface identifiers offer alternatives to MAC-derived identifiers; actual use depends on platform and configuration.')
q[5]['prompt']='Peer A\'s NAT uses address-and-port-dependent mapping AND filtering. A STUN server sees A as `198.51.100.4:50123`, but A\'s mapping towards Peer B uses a different port. All direct ICE candidate checks fail, while an authenticated TURN-relayed candidate succeeds. What explains the STUN candidate failure and the usable alternative?'
q[5]['options'][0]['explanation']='STUN supports multiple transports; in this scenario it correctly reports the mapping observed by that server. It does not promise that mapping is usable by B.'
q[5]['options'][1]['text']='The STUN-observed mapping is not a usable path for B under these mapping/filtering rules; ICE can nominate the working relayed candidate'
q[5]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Both the distinct mapping and filtering assumptions are given. Destination-dependent mapping alone would not prove that every direct path fails.'
q[5]['options'][3]['explanation']='The successful STUN exchange contradicts a blanket UDP block in this scenario. ICE checks gathered candidates; an external mapping-control mechanism is separate.'
q[5]['workedExample']='The NAT allocated a mapping for the STUN destination and filters that mapping to its permitted peer. A different B mapping therefore cannot be reached merely by reusing the server-observed address. The question explicitly says all direct checks fail and a relayed check succeeds, so ICE can use that valid relay pair. NAT classification alone is insufficient to infer this outcome for every topology.'
q[6]['options'][0]['text']='Private addresses embedded in SIP or active FTP payloads may be unusable by an external peer'
q[6]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. A header-only translation does not repair embedded addresses. Application traversal, relays or suitable ALGs may handle them.'
q[6]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Multiple subscribers can share a public address, so an address-wide restriction can affect unrelated subscribers; the sharing ratio is deployment-specific.'
q[6]['options'][3]['text']='Losing unsynchronised dynamic NAT state can interrupt existing connections'
q[6]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct. Active return mappings may disappear. Replication, preserved state or static mappings can alter this behaviour.'
q[6]['options'][4]['text']='Attributing shared-address traffic to a subscriber needs mapping records with enough protocol, port and time detail'
q[6]['options'][4]['explanation']='Correct. A public address alone can represent several subscribers. Required fields depend on the mapping policy; records do not prove which individual person sent traffic.'
q[6]['workedExample']='Embedded addresses can need application-aware handling. Shared public addresses complicate address-based blocking and subscriber attribution. Dynamic translation state introduces failure and expiry cases. NAT by itself rewrites addressing rather than encrypting application data. The relevant outcomes depend on configuration, state management and logging detail.'
q[7]['prompt']='A Happy Eyeballs client has both address families ready, starts IPv6 first, uses a 250 ms attempt delay, and has a working IPv4 path. IPv6 connection attempts receive no response. What happens at about 250 ms if the IPv6 attempt is still pending?'
q[7]['options'][2]['text']='It starts an IPv4 connection attempt without waiting for the IPv6 attempt to time out'
q[7]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct under the stated configuration. IPv4 still needs its own connection and application work; this does not fix total page-load time at 250 ms.'
q[7]['workedExample']='The configured timer permits another candidate attempt at about 250 ms while IPv6 is pending. A successful IPv4 connection can then be used. RFC 8305 recommends this default delay but permits adaptive behaviour; DNS timing, address order, immediate errors and implementations can change the sequence.'
q[8]['prompt']='As a bit-encoding exercise, append documentation IPv4 address `192.0.2.33` to prefix `64:ff9b::/96`. What IPv6 representation results? This is not a valid public NAT64 translation example: RFC 6052 prohibits this well-known prefix for non-global IPv4 addresses.'
q[8]['options'][1]['explanation']='That is an IPv4-mapped socket-API representation, not the requested /96 prefix encoding.'
q[8]['options'][3]['text']='No 128-bit address can encode those bits'
q[8]['options'][3]['explanation']='The bit representation exists. Translation policy is a separate constraint; the question explicitly excludes a working public NAT64 deployment.'
q[8]['workedExample']='192.0.2.33 is the byte sequence c0 00 02 21, or the two groups c000:0221. Appending it to the specified /96 produces `64:ff9b::c000:221`. This demonstrates representation only: RFC 6052 forbids using the well-known prefix to translate non-global addresses, including documentation space. A real example must use eligible addressing and a configured translator.'
q[9]['prompt']='A firewall drops all inbound ICMPv6 on a newly configured Ethernet host. Which functions depend on messages this rule blocks? Select all that apply.'
q[9]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. NS/NA are ICMPv6. Without a usable cached/static mapping or other mechanism, rejecting advertisements prevents normal neighbour resolution.'
q[9]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Prefix information for SLAAC and default-router discovery arrive in Router Advertisements. Address configuration and default-route learning are distinct functions.'
q[9]['options'][2]['text']='Receiving router MTU feedback for classical path MTU discovery'
q[9]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. Packet Too Big is ICMPv6. Packetisation-layer probing may recover without those messages, so not every large transfer must fail.'
q[9]['workedExample']='Inbound ICMPv6 includes neighbour advertisements, router advertisements and Packet Too Big messages. Filtering all of it removes those inputs. Static/cached configuration or packetisation-layer probing may mask some failures, but do not make the rule harmless. An ICMPv6-only rule does not directly match IPv4 packets. RFC 4890 discusses filtering by message role.'
q[10]['prompt']='A VoIP app uses UDP behind IPv4 NAPT. Incoming calls fail after idle periods. A capture and NAT-state inspection confirm that the relevant mapping expires before the incoming request. Which response addresses this confirmed cause?'
q[10]['options'][0]['text']='Use protocol-appropriate keepalives and/or adjust the permitted NAT policy based on the measured timeout and refresh behaviour'
q[10]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Outbound traffic can refresh the mapping, subject to NAT behaviour. Choose timing from evidence and protocol requirements, rather than assuming one universal consumer-router timer.'
q[10]['options'][1]['explanation']='Changing a DNS TTL does not refresh the confirmed expired translation mapping.'
q[10]['options'][2]['explanation']='Switching transport alone does not guarantee state will remain available; TCP state can also expire and application/server state matters.'
q[10]['options'][3]['explanation']='This is a confirmed IPv4 translation-state failure, not an IPv6 address-lifetime diagnosis.'
q[10]['workedExample']='The observation establishes that the mapping disappeared, rather than merely suggesting it from idle-time symptoms. Check which packets refresh the mapping and coordinate application keepalives or allowed NAT policy accordingly. RFC 4787 specifies a two-minute minimum UDP timer with a defined exception, but its requirement is not evidence of the timer on this device. Registration expiry and other server state still require separate handling.'
q[11]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct for the stated ordinary forwarding case, subject to ICMPv6 generation/rate rules. IPv6 strongly recommends PMTUD; a minimal sender can instead stay within the 1,280-byte minimum link MTU.'
q[11]['workedExample']='The 1,600-byte IPv6 packet exceeds the 1,500-byte outgoing MTU. An IPv6 forwarding router does not fragment it; it discards it and reports Packet Too Big with MTU 1,500, subject to error-generation/rate rules. The source can adjust packetisation or source fragmentation. IP does not itself guarantee retransmission; recovery depends on the transport/application.'
p.write_text(json.dumps(q,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
